<Project Sdk="Aspire.AppHost.Sdk/13.6.1">

  <!--
    **The factory's host, and the one place in this repository where Aspire's version
    lives (#63).**

    `Aspire.AppHost.Sdk` is an MSBuild SDK rather than a `PackageReference`, and that is
    the whole reason this file is worth a comment: `dotnet add package` cannot update an
    `Sdk=` attribute, so the version is pinned here, in one place, at the version the
    research found current — **13.6.1** (released 2026-10-07; Aspire ships a minor a month
    and each minor ends support of the previous one, so every 9.x release has been
    unsupported since 2025-11-11). The SDK implicitly adds `Aspire.Hosting.AppHost` at the
    same number, so there is deliberately no `PackageReference` for it: adding one is the
    documented way to make the two disagree, and the runtime version check reads the *SDK*
    number, so a skew fails at launch with "Newer version of Aspire.Hosting.AppHost
    required". Raising Aspire is `aspire update`, or an edit here and a look at the
    packages the next build resolves.

    **Why this project is under `src/` and is still not one of the factory's four.** The
    four projects that are the factory — `Domain`, `Application`, `Infrastructure`, `Web`
    — are what `docs/architecture.md` calls the dependency rule's graph, what
    `ArchitectureTests` asserts the edges of, and what `Boundary/TheFactory` names as the
    four assemblies `PolicyTests`' IL scans read. This project is none of those: it names
    no factory type but the one it launches, holds no policy, and is the outermost thing
    in the repository. It sits in `src/` beside them because `src/` is where this
    repository's source lives — one source folder and one test folder at the root, the
    layout a solution generated with the Aspire AppHost selected has — and
    `ArchitectureTests.TheFactoryProjects` skips it by name, so it stays a fifth *project*
    and never becomes a fifth assembly a structural scan absorbs. See the Hosting section
    of `docs/architecture.md`.

    **`ASPIRE010` is suppressed, deliberately and visibly.** The warning says this project
    is configured with `AspireUseCliBundle=false` and that "some Aspire features require
    the Aspire CLI bundle". Nothing this AppHost uses is one of them: the orchestration
    (DCP) and the dashboard come from the RID-specific `Aspire.Hosting.Orchestration.*` and
    `Aspire.Dashboard.Sdk.*` packages the SDK restores, which is the documented default.
    Turning the bundle on is only possible with the `aspire` CLI installed — standalone
    tooling this repository does not add to the machine — so `true` would fail the build
    with a missing tool rather than fix anything. This repository builds with zero
    warnings, so the honest form of "not applicable here" is a suppression with this
    sentence beside it rather than a warning everyone learns to ignore.

    `MessagePack` is lifted to its patched release by the `<PackageVersion>` in
    `Directory.Packages.props` together with the central transitive pinning below — **not**
    by a `PackageReference` here; the reason, and why suppressing the audit instead would be
    wrong, is in that file.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);ASPIRE010</NoWarn>
    <!--
      **Central transitive pinning, so the package lifted in `Directory.Packages.props` can be
      lifted without being referenced.** `MessagePack` is Aspire's, not this project's, and a
      direct `PackageReference` to a package an AppHost never names would be a dependency nobody is
      reviewing — the same shape as the `YamlDotNet` reference `Web` carried for a project
      loader that had already moved away. This property is the documented way to say "pin the
      transitive version" instead: the `PackageVersion` in `Directory.Packages.props` is the
      whole mechanism, and it is scoped to this project rather than turned on for the
      repository, because the four factory projects have no reason to pin transitives they do
      not name.
    -->
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The one project reference, and it is what makes the resource graph a compile-time
    fact rather than a string: the SDK's source generator turns it into
    `Projects.AgentFactory_Web`, whose `ProjectPath` is this path. `Program` then asks for
    `AddProject<Projects.AgentFactory_Web>(...)` with no path in sight, so a rename or a
    move cannot leave the graph pointing at a project that is no longer there.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\AgentFactory.Web\AgentFactory.Web.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
